The audit command writes each cause's report IDs and a CSV, so each issue's count is reproducible.

## Parser: month-first interpretation for day-first tenants

The shared parser treats numeric dates as month first. A tenant whose locale is day first can submit
`07/04/2026` for 7 April and receive 4 July. Tenant settings, rather than the reporter's browser locale,
determine how the date should be read.

Reproduce with a day-first tenant on the app or email path, or the booking-v2 form.
Capture the entered date, stored date and generated invite before changing a booking.
The expected result preserves the date intended under the tenant's locale.

The parser issue covers every report the supplied classifier calls `parser`, including the explicitly flagged
one-day descriptions. Keep that attribution qualified until their original inputs and timestamps are checked.

## Hint: booking-v3 instructs the opposite date order

For day-first tenants, booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, while its form reads dates in the tenant's day-first order.
For example, a user follows that hint and enters `04/07/2026` for 7 April; a day-first form reads 4 July.
Before retirement, the field instruction and its examples needed to agree with the form parser.
Changing the shared parser without checking this form would not prove that the hint problem was fixed.
The desk instead reports the form retired and the hint outcome as won't fix.

Reproduce on the form channel for Alder Physio (`de-DE`), Copper Studio (`es-ES`) or Pale Harbour (`pt-PT`).
Check the hint shown, original input and parsed value. App and email reports from those tenants still use the
parser classification. A tenant name alone is insufficient to group a report.

## What the timeline measures

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Read received and merged bars separately. A large merge bar can collect older reports.
Later reports continue entering the known ticket, so “known” is a queue label, not evidence of recovery.
Report counts are not counts of affected users or proof of an incident's complete scope.

## Triage after this review

The [accepted subject-only decision](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) is preserved as historical evidence.
Its age and the live examples below make it unsafe as a cause test.
The [proposed replacement](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) explains how to group by mechanism and record uncertainty.

<DedupeExplorer />

Same tenant and subject can conceal different causes when the channel changes. Different subjects can share
one cause. An unknown tenant cannot be safely assigned a cause; look up its locale and form before merging.

## Follow-up and ownership

The closed [parser issue](https://github.com/victorwhite5/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) and
[hint issue](https://github.com/victorwhite5/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2) each carry their export count,
report IDs, reproduction steps, verification criteria and their distinct engineering outcomes.
Keep the one-day descriptions visible if the desk receives further timing failures after the parser fix.
Before future triage decisions, rebuild from a fresh desk export and check any new support evidence in those issues.

